# Code of Ethics and Conduct

**{{ org_name }}** -- a 501(c)(3) nonprofit research institute incorporated in {{ state }} (EIN: {{ ein }})

## 1. Purpose

{{ org_name }} ("the Organization") exists to advance scientific research in the public interest. This Code of Ethics and Conduct establishes the ethical standards and professional expectations that apply to every person who acts on behalf of the Organization. It serves as the foundation upon which all other organizational policies rest and reflects the Organization's obligation, as a tax-exempt entity and a recipient of federal awards, to maintain the highest standards of integrity and accountability.

## 2. Scope

This Code applies to all directors, officers, employees, contractors, consultants, volunteers, fellows, and trainees of the Organization (collectively, "Covered Persons"). Covered Persons are expected to read, understand, and comply with this Code and to raise questions or concerns when in doubt.

## 3. Core Principles

All Covered Persons shall conduct themselves in accordance with these principles:

- **Integrity** -- act honestly and ethically in all professional dealings; do not deceive, manipulate, or misrepresent.
- **Honesty** -- provide truthful and complete information in all reports, records, communications, and filings.
- **Transparency** -- operate openly; support the Organization's ability to account for its activities to the board, funders, regulators, and the public.
- **Accountability** -- accept responsibility for decisions and actions; acknowledge and correct errors promptly.
- **Respect** -- treat all individuals with dignity regardless of role, background, or status; foster a professional environment free from intimidation or hostility.
- **Stewardship** -- protect and use organizational resources, including federal award funds, responsibly and solely for their intended purposes.

## 4. Research Integrity

The Organization's credibility depends on the integrity of its research. Covered Persons involved in research activities must not engage in fabrication, falsification, or plagiarism as defined in the Organization's **Research Misconduct Policy**. All research data, methodologies, and results must be recorded accurately and reported honestly. Covered Persons must complete responsible conduct of research training as required by the **RCR Training Policy**.

## 5. Conflicts of Interest

Covered Persons must disclose any actual or potential conflict of interest in accordance with the Organization's **Conflict of Interest Policy**. Investigators must additionally comply with the **Financial Conflict of Interest Policy (Investigator)** when engaged in federally funded research. No Covered Person shall use their position for improper personal benefit or to benefit family members or business associates at the expense of the Organization.

## 6. Confidentiality

Covered Persons must protect confidential information belonging to the Organization, its research partners, sponsors, and subjects. This includes proprietary research data, financial records, personnel information, trade secrets of STTR/SBIR partners, and information protected under data use agreements. Confidential information shall not be disclosed to unauthorized parties or used for personal advantage. Obligations survive separation from the Organization.

## 7. Use of Organizational Resources

Organizational resources -- including funds, equipment, facilities, intellectual property, and information systems -- shall be used only for authorized purposes. No Covered Person may use federal award funds for personal expenditures. All use of organizational computing resources must comply with the **Cybersecurity Policy**. Incidental personal use of non-federal resources is permitted only when it is minimal, does not interfere with duties, and does not violate any other policy.

## 8. Financial Responsibility

Covered Persons involved in financial transactions or reporting must ensure that all records are accurate, complete, and maintained in accordance with generally accepted accounting principles and the **Cost Accounting Policy**. No person shall engage in fraud, embezzlement, or intentional misstatement of financial records. Expenditures of federal funds must comply with the cost principles in 2 CFR Part 200, Subpart E. Suspected financial irregularities must be reported immediately.

## 9. Non-Discrimination and Harassment

The Organization prohibits discrimination and harassment in all forms, as set forth in the **Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity Policy**. Covered Persons shall not engage in conduct that discriminates on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, age, religion, veteran status, genetic information, or any other protected characteristic. Harassment -- including sexual harassment -- will not be tolerated.

## 10. Compliance with Laws

Covered Persons must comply with all applicable federal, state, and local laws and regulations, including but not limited to:

- Tax-exempt organization requirements under IRC Section 501(c)(3)
- Federal award administration requirements (2 CFR Part 200)
- Export control regulations (EAR and ITAR), as detailed in the **Export Control Policy**
- Anti-lobbying restrictions applicable to federal award recipients
- Suspension and debarment regulations (2 CFR Part 180)

Ignorance of the law is not a defense. Covered Persons are expected to seek guidance when uncertain about legal obligations.

## 11. Responsible AI Use

The Organization integrates artificial intelligence throughout its research and operations. Covered Persons must use AI tools responsibly and in compliance with the **Responsible AI Use Policy for Research Activities** and the **AI Governance Policy for Board Operations**. AI shall not be used to fabricate data, forge citations, generate fraudulent results, or misrepresent AI-generated content as solely human-authored work in contexts where disclosure is required. All AI use in federally funded research must be documented and disclosed as required by the applicable sponsor.

## 12. Partner Relationships

Covered Persons must deal fairly and honestly with all external partners, including STTR/SBIR small business concerns, subrecipients, collaborators, vendors, and federal agencies. The Organization's obligations under teaming agreements, CRADAs, subaward agreements, and consortium arrangements must be honored. No Covered Person shall take unfair advantage of a partner through manipulation, concealment, misrepresentation, or abuse of privileged information.

## 13. Whistleblower Protection

The Organization encourages the prompt reporting of suspected violations of this Code, organizational policies, or applicable law. Reports may be made through the channels described in the **Whistleblower Protection Policy**. Retaliation against any person who makes a good-faith report is strictly prohibited and will itself be treated as a violation of this Code. Anonymous reports will be accepted and investigated.

## 14. Political Activity

As a 501(c)(3) organization, {{ org_name }} is absolutely prohibited from participating or intervening in any political campaign on behalf of or in opposition to any candidate for public office. No organizational resources -- including staff time, email systems, mailing lists, or funds -- shall be used for political campaign activity. Covered Persons retain their personal right to participate in the political process on their own time and with their own resources, but must not represent personal political views as those of the Organization.

## 15. Enforcement

Violations of this Code may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment, removal from the board, termination of a contract or volunteer relationship, or referral to appropriate legal authorities. The severity of the response will be proportionate to the nature and gravity of the violation. Determinations of violations and appropriate sanctions will be made by the Board of Directors or its designee following a fair review process.

## 16. Annual Acknowledgment

All Covered Persons must acknowledge receipt of and agreement to comply with this Code upon joining the Organization and annually thereafter. The Executive Director (or Board Chair, in the absence of an Executive Director) is responsible for distributing this Code and collecting signed acknowledgments. Failure to submit a timely acknowledgment does not relieve a Covered Person of their obligation to comply.


## 17. Adoption

This policy becomes effective on merge to `main`; the merge commit and MR approval record are the official adoption record.
